.TH LSATTR 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
lsattr \- list Windows file attributes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsattr
.RB [ \-Radl ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsattr
lists the Windows file attributes of each
.IR file ,
such as read-only, hidden, and compressed.
With no
.IR file ,
it lists the current directory.
.BR chattr (1)
changes some of the attributes.
.PP
A
.I file
that is a directory is not listed itself.
Its entries are listed instead, without descending into subdirectories;
see
.B \-d
and
.BR \-R .
A symbolic link or junction is listed itself, never its target or the
entries of its target, even when it is named on the command line,
unless that name ends in a slash or backslash.
A junction, or a symbolic link to a directory, named that way, as in
.IR jn/ ,
is treated as a plain directory: its entries are listed, and with
.B \-d
its first attribute position shows
.B R
and
.B \-l
shows
.BR Reparse_Point .
.PP
Each line of output is an attribute field, a space, and a name.
The name of a
.I file
is printed as given.
The name of an entry in a directory is the name of the directory, a
slash, and the name of the entry, with the slash omitted if the
directory's name already ends in one or is a bare drive, as in
.IR C:main.c .
A trailing backslash does not count: the entries of
.I proj\e
are named like
.IR proj\e/main.c .
Names are never converted, so backslashes and drive letters stay as
typed.
Entries are listed in the order the file system returns them;
.B lsattr
does not sort.
Entries whose names begin with a dot are skipped unless
.B \-a
is given.
The hidden and system attributes do not hide an entry.
.PP
Files are processed in order.
A file that cannot be found, or an entry that cannot be examined, is
reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
lsattr: can\(aqt stat \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and the remaining files are still processed.
A device, such as
.IR /dev/null ,
has no attributes and is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
lsattr: reading \fIfile\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
This message may end with a reason left over from an earlier failure,
which does not apply.
A directory that cannot be opened is skipped without a message.
.SS Attribute field
The field has one position for each attribute shown, in the order below.
A position holds the attribute's letter if the file has the attribute and
a hyphen if it does not, so a file with none of them shows 11 hyphens.
.TP
.BR j ", " l ", " A ", " R
The first position.
It is
.B j
for a junction,
.B l
for a symbolic link,
.B A
for an app execution link, such as those in
.IR $LOCALAPPDATA/Microsoft/WindowsApps ,
and
.B R
for any other reparse point, such as a cloud storage placeholder.
.TP
.B o
Offline.
.TP
.B e
Encrypted.
.TP
.B c
Compressed.
.TP
.B S
Sparse.
.TP
.B r
Read-only.
.TP
.B h
Hidden.
.TP
.B s
System.
.TP
.B a
Archive.
.TP
.B t
Temporary.
.TP
.B n
Not content indexed.
.PP
No other attribute is shown.
A directory has no letter of its own.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-aR ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options, for a
.I file
whose name begins with a dash.
.TP
.B \-R
Recurse: list the entries of each subdirectory found in a directory
that is listed.
After the line for the subdirectory,
.B lsattr
prints a blank line, the subdirectory's name followed by a colon, the
subdirectory's entries, and another blank line, and then goes on with
the rest of the parent's entries.
The directory named on the command line gets no such heading.
The
.B .\&
and
.B ..\&
entries, symbolic links, and junctions are not entered, and a
subdirectory whose name begins with a dot is entered only with
.BR \-a .
The heading of a subdirectory that cannot be opened is still printed,
followed by no entries.
With
.BR \-d ,
nothing is entered.
.TP
.B \-a
List entries whose names begin with a dot, including the
.B .\&
and
.B ..\&
entries.
Both are listed in every directory, even the root directory of a drive,
where the file system does not return them.
.TP
.B \-d
List a directory named on the command line itself, instead of its
entries.
.TP
.B \-l
Print the names of the attributes instead of the attribute field.
Each line is the file's name padded with spaces to 28 bytes, a space,
and the names of the attributes the file has, separated by a comma and a
space, in the order of the attribute field.
A name of 28 bytes or more is followed by just the one space.
The names, in that order, are
.BR Reparse_Point ,
.BR Offline ,
.BR Encrypted ,
.BR Compressed ,
.BR Sparse ,
.BR Read_Only ,
.BR Hidden ,
.BR System ,
.BR Archive ,
.BR Temporary ,
and
.BR Not_Indexed .
A symbolic link, junction, or app execution link shows
.BR Symbolic_Link ,
.BR Junction ,
or
.B App_Exec_Link
instead of
.BR Reparse_Point .
A file with none of the attributes shows
.BR \-\-\- .
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that busybox-w32 reads in every applet, such as those that
control console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
Files that could not be found or listed do not affect the status.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, with a usage message on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the attributes of a directory itself, and of a file in it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lsattr \-d proj proj/main.c
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- proj
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-a\-\- proj/main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
List a directory tree.
The listing of a subdirectory comes right after its own line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lsattr \-R proj
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- proj/include
\&
proj/include:
\-\-\-\-\-r\-\-a\-\- proj/include/util.h
\&
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-a\-\- proj/main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show attribute names instead of letters:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lsattr \-l proj/include/util.h
proj/include/util.h          Read_Only, Archive
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the files with the hidden attribute in a tree, dot files included:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lsattr \-aR | grep \(aq^......h.... \(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find the symbolic links and junctions in a tree:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lsattr \-R src | grep \(aq^[jl]\(aq
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the e2fsprogs
.B lsattr
on Linux:
.IP \(bu 2
The attributes are Windows file attributes, not ext2 inode flags, and
the letters mean different things:
.B a
is archive, not append-only, and
.B s
is system, not secure deletion.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.B \-p
or
.BR \-v ;
both are invalid options.
.IP \(bu 2
Errors on individual files do not change the exit status.
.PP
Of the attributes that
.B lsattr
shows,
.BR chattr (1)
can change only
.BR r ,
.BR h ,
.BR s ,
.BR a ,
.BR t ,
and
.BR n .
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
A bare drive such as
.I C:
names the current directory of that drive.
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
A path of 260 or more characters, counting the current directory when the
path is relative, cannot be examined and is reported as
.B can\(aqt stat
like a missing file.
.PP
The padding for
.B \-l
counts bytes, not characters, so a name with non-ASCII characters is
followed by fewer spaces.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR chattr (1),
.BR ls (1)
